  [PASS] R4 [robust] the T2 verdict is the SAME in every halo row above (log M_200 +- 0.6 dex = 2x the abundance-matching uncertainty, log c +- 0.22 dex = 2x Dutton-Maccio's scatter, the cosmic-ratio halo, the Lelli cuts), so the conclusion does not flip inside the halo uncertainty and the test IS decisive with current data   (all 11 halo rows agree with the primary (no scale survives); the two no-halo controls both go the other way, so the scan is alive)
  [PASS] R4b [margin] a_0 returns only if the halo is pushed more than 3x the abundance-matching uncertainty (0.90 dex) BELOW what LambdaCDM predicts, so the null has margin   (a_0 comes back only at log M_200 -1.80 dex, i.e. a halo 63x smaller than LambdaCDM's and 6.0 sigma below it)

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT
======================================================================================================================
  granting every SPARC galaxy the cold halo LambdaCDM predicts for its stellar mass (Moster+ 2013 abundance
  matching, Dutton & Maccio 2014 concentration, NFW, baryons subtracted so nothing is double counted), the
  residual B = g_obs/(g_bar + g_halo) is
    NOT ORGANISED BY ACCELERATION -- the halo did the work:
    a curve in g_bar alone removes -3.5% of the variance a constant leaves (74.0% with no halo),
    and the fit returns NO acceleration scale (fit railed at the search floor 1e-13.5; bootstrap 95% upper limit a_eff < 3.29e-13 m/s^2).
  This is not a sensitivity failure: A2 recovers an injected a_0 to 0.100 dex, and the detection
    floor is a_0 x 10^-0.50 = 2.96e-11 m/s^2, so any surviving scale above that would have been seen.
  The overall normalisation offset is -0.044 dex: the granted halo OVER-supplies
    the observed acceleration by 10% on average, which is the price of granting it,
    and it does so with 0.171 dex of scatter against the fixed-a_0 kernel's 0.142 dex -- so the kernel
    remains the TIGHTER description even though it is no longer the REQUIRED one.
  What this lane does NOT show: it does not measure dark matter; it does not by itself rescue clusters; and the
  halo used is the plain equilibrium NFW population with no adiabatic contraction and no feedback -- both of which
  move inner profiles and are named here as the leading systematic on H1 and on the offset above.  Nor does it
  touch the programme's distinctive prediction a_0 = kappa c sqrt(G rho_Lambda), which is a claim about the VALUE
  of an acceleration scale and is tested by the pre-registered measurements, not by this rotation-curve inversion.

RESULT: 12 checks, 5 FAIL -> ["H1 [control] baryons + the abundance-matched NFW halo with NO boost reproduce the inner rotation curves to the RAR's own 0.11 dex", 'T2 [THE TEST] after granting a LambdaCDM cold halo the residual boost is STILL organised by acceleration (a g_bar-only curve removes >= 50% of the variance a constant leaves, AND the fitted scale is not vanishing)', 'T3 [scale] the acceleration scale surviving the abundance-matched halo agrees with a_0 to within 0.15 dex on at least one footing', 'A3 [within-galaxy] the acceleration structure survives the halo INSIDE individual galaxies (the median per-galaxy slope keeps at least half its no-halo value)', "A4 [scatter] the LambdaCDM halo population still matches the rotation curves once its own scatter in M_200 and concentration is switched on, i.e. within the RAR's observed 0.13 dex"]
